      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-2">Updated November 11, 2026: the plan has been released</p>
        <p class="text-amber-800">We first published this page on October 14, 2026, before the plan was tabled. IRCC released the 2027–2029 Immigration Levels Plan on November 5, 2026, and this page now reports the published figures from IRCC's supplementary information and backgrounder. Comparisons with the previous plan use the notional 2027 figures from the 2026–2028 plan. Anything about what the plan "may" mean for BC is our analysis, clearly labelled.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What the Levels Plan is, and when it arrived</h2>

      <p>
        Every year the federal immigration minister must table an Annual Report to Parliament on Immigration. Under section 94 of the <em>Immigration and Refugee Protection Act</em>, the report must include the number of people projected to become permanent residents in the following year. In practice it contains a three-year plan. The first year is a firm target, and the next two are "notional" targets that are revisited a year later.
      </p>
      <p>
        The deadline is set in law: on or before <strong>November 1</strong>, or, if a House of Parliament is not sitting then, within the next 30 days on which that House sits. November 1, 2026 fell on a Sunday. IRCC published the 2027–2029 plan on <strong>November 5, 2026</strong>, along with its supplementary information tables and a backgrounder.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The 2027–2029 permanent resident targets</h2>

      <p>
        The table below shows the published targets, with the 2027 low–high range in brackets, next to the notional 2027 figure from the previous (2026–2028) plan.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Category</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">2027 target (range)</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">2028</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">2029</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Previous plan, 2027</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr class="font-semibold bg-slate-50"><td class="px-4 py-3">Overall PR admissions</td><td class="px-4 py-3">380,000 (352,500–422,000)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">380,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">380,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">380,000</td></tr>
            <tr class="font-semibold"><td class="px-4 py-3">Economic total</td><td class="px-4 py-3">244,700 (230,000–270,000)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">244,700</td><td class="px-4 py-3">244,700</td><td class="px-4 py-3">244,700</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">Federal High Skilled (Express Entry)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">100,000 (87,000–123,000)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">100,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">100,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">111,000</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-green-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8"><strong>Provincial Nominee Program</strong></td><td class="px-4 py-3"><strong>95,000 (83,000–106,000)</strong></td><td class="px-4 py-3"><strong>95,000</strong></td><td class="px-4 py-3"><strong>95,000</strong></td><td class="px-4 py-3"><strong>92,500</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">Federal economic pilots</td><td class="px-4 py-3">7,900 (5,000–15,450)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6,175</td><td class="px-4 py-3">7,400</td><td class="px-4 py-3">8,775</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">Rural Community Immigration Program</td><td class="px-4 py-3">5,500 (4,000–6,500)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6,500</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No separate line</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">Atlantic Immigration Program</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6,000 (5,000–8,500)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">4,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">Federal Business</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,500 (500–5,000)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2,725</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">500</td></tr>
            <tr class="font-semibold"><td class="px-4 py-3">Family total</td><td class="px-4 py-3">81,000 (76,000–89,000)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">81,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">81,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">81,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">Spouses, partners and children</td><td class="px-4 py-3">66,000 (61,000–75,000)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">66,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">66,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">66,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">Parents and grandparents</td><td class="px-4 py-3">15,000 (12,000–18,000)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">15,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">15,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">15,000</td></tr>
            <tr class="font-semibold"><td class="px-4 py-3">Refugees and protected persons</td><td class="px-4 py-3">49,300 (42,000–55,000)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">49,300</td><td class="px-4 py-3">49,300</td><td class="px-4 py-3">49,300</td></tr>
            <tr class="font-semibold"><td class="px-4 py-3">Humanitarian and other</td><td class="px-4 py-3">5,000 (4,500–8,000)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">5,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">5,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">5,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Francophone admissions outside Quebec</td><td class="px-4 py-3">9.5% (31,825)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">10.5% (35,175)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">12% (40,200)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">9.5% (31,825)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Quebec's skilled worker and business numbers are again shown as "TBD" because Quebec sets its own targets under the Canada–Quebec Accord. IRCC lists the Rural Community Immigration Program on its own row; on November 9, 2026 it announced that the former rural pilot is now a permanent program. The 2028 and 2029 ranges for most lines are in IRCC's supplementary tables.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">What changed from the previous plan</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>PNP up 2,500.</strong> 95,000 a year instead of the notional 92,500 for 2027. The figure is held flat to 2029.</li>
        <li><strong>Express Entry down 11,000.</strong> Federal High Skilled falls from a notional 111,000 to 100,000, so a larger share of economic admissions goes through provinces.</li>
        <li><strong>Atlantic and rural up.</strong> The Atlantic Immigration Program rises to 6,000 and the permanent Rural Community Immigration Program gets 5,500 in 2027.</li>
        <li><strong>Totals unchanged.</strong> Overall PR, economic, family and refugee totals match the previous plan's notional 2027 figures.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">A one-time 2027 measure for people already in Canada</h3>
      <p>
        The new plan adds a one-time measure in 2027 for up to <strong>200,000 people already in Canada</strong>. IRCC's November 10, 2026 release breaks it down as follows and says it aims to finish all of these applications by the end of 2027:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Group</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Up to</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr class="bg-green-50"><td class="px-4 py-3">Workers and families, with provinces and territories (including expanded PNP allocations)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">100,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">In-Canada spouses, partners and children</td><td class="px-4 py-3">45,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Hong Kong pathways (closed to new applications August 31, 2026)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">32,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Ukraine Family Reunification Pathway</td><td class="px-4 py-3">18,800</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">People already found eligible on humanitarian and compassionate grounds</td><td class="px-4 py-3">12,600</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The workers cohort also covers pending Atlantic Immigration Program applications and people in closed programs (Start-up Visa, Self-Employed, the Agri-Food Pilot and the home care worker programs). IRCC's supplementary page does not say whether these admissions fall inside the 380,000 target or on top of it. The 2026–2028 plan's earlier one-time measures (about 115,000 protected persons over two years, and up to 33,000 work permit holders in 2026 and 2027) were announced separately.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Temporary resident arrivals</h3>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">New arrivals</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">2026 (previous plan)</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">2027 (range)</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">2028 and 2029</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr class="font-semibold"><td class="px-4 py-3">Total temporary arrivals</td><td class="px-4 py-3">385,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">370,000 (355,000–385,000)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">370,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Workers</td><td class="px-4 py-3">230,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">220,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">220,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">International Mobility Program</td><td class="px-4 py-3">170,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">170,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">170,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">Temporary Foreign Worker Program</td><td class="px-4 py-3">60,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">50,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">50,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Students</td><td class="px-4 py-3">155,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">150,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">150,000</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The 2027 figures match the previous plan's notional 2027 numbers, and the plan holds them flat for 2028 and 2029.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The pressure point: the temporary resident population</h2>

      <p>
        Before this plan, the government's stated goal was to bring temporary residents below 5% of Canada's population by the end of 2027. The 2027–2029 plan says the temporary resident population will be reduced, but does not give a new numeric target. Progress so far:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>IRCC told a parliamentary committee in April 2026 that the share had come down from a peak of 7.6% to 6.5% (as of January 1, 2026).</li>
        <li>Statistics Canada's preliminary second-quarter estimate, released September 23, 2026, put the number of non-permanent residents at 2,779,774 on July 1, 2026, out of a total population of 41,798,407. That works out to about 6.7%. Statistics Canada notes that preliminary figures are revised later.</li>
        <li>In British Columbia, the same release counted 465,514 non-permanent residents out of 5,710,598 people on July 1, 2026, about 8.2% by our calculation. That is higher than the national share.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Our analysis:</strong> going from about 6.7% to under 5% in roughly a year would mean well over 600,000 fewer temporary residents at today's population size. The plan did not cut new temporary arrivals below the 370,000 already pencilled in for 2027. Instead it leans on transitions: the one-time 2027 measure moves up to 200,000 people already in Canada to permanent residence, including up to 100,000 workers and families with provinces and territories. For people already in Canada on work permits, that makes 2027 a year when an in-Canada PR route may be more available than usual, but there is still no general guarantee of status. We cover this in our <a href="/articles/canada-temporary-resident-cap-2027-bc-work-permit-holders" class="text-blue-600 underline">temporary resident cap guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What Canadians and stakeholders were asked</h2>

      <p>
        IRCC's consultation for this plan ran from <strong>May 12 to June 30, 2026</strong>. This year's survey switched to open-ended questions. The published questions show the commitments the department treats as settled. Question 19 lists three:
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Reducing the temporary population below 5% of the total by the end of 2027</li>
        <li>Stabilizing permanent resident admissions below 1% of the population after 2027</li>
        <li>Raising the Francophone admissions target outside Quebec to 12% by 2029</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The other open questions asked about regional pressures and demographic trends (Q20), priorities beyond 2029 (Q21) and barriers to good outcomes (Q22). The consultation page lists priorities including refugee protection, family reunification, Francophone immigration outside Quebec, and matching immigration levels to housing, infrastructure and public services capacity, as well as filling labour gaps.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Why the "below 1%" line matters:</strong> 1% of a population of about 41.8 million is about 418,000. A PR target of 380,000 sits under that line. The new plan keeps PR at 380,000 through 2029 and says admissions will stay below 1% of the population beyond 2027. It also confirms the 12% Francophone target for 2029.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The seven things we said to check, and what the plan shows</h2>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-4">
        <li><strong>The overall 2027 PR target.</strong> Confirmed at 380,000, with the same figure for 2028 and 2029.</li>
        <li><strong>The PNP line for 2027 and the first figure for 2029.</strong> Raised to 95,000 for 2027 and held at 95,000 for 2028 and 2029. This is the number that matters most for provincial programs, and it gives room for larger allocations than the 92,500 notional figure.</li>
        <li><strong>Federal High Skilled.</strong> Cut to 100,000 from a notional 111,000, so provinces carry more of the economic share.</li>
        <li><strong>Temporary arrival targets.</strong> Kept at 370,000 for 2027 (220,000 workers: 170,000 International Mobility Program and 50,000 TFWP), and held there for 2028 and 2029.</li>
        <li><strong>New or extended one-time initiatives.</strong> Yes: a one-time 2027 measure for up to 200,000 people already in Canada, half of them workers and families with provinces and territories.</li>
        <li><strong>Francophone targets.</strong> 9.5% for 2027, 10.5% for 2028 and 12% for 2029, as signalled. This affects French-speaking candidates in every province; see our <a href="/articles/canada-pr-planning-guide-french-advantage-2026" class="text-blue-600 underline">French advantage guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Supplementary notes on PNP allocations.</strong> The plan sets the national total and says provinces will nominate workers already in Canada under expanded PNP allocations. The provincial split still comes later, when IRCC notifies each province.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">From national target to BC's allocation</h2>

      <p>
        A national PNP number does not become BC nominations on the same day. IRCC first tables the plan, then notifies each province of its allocation, and may add mid-year increases. BC's last two cycles show how long that takes:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Date</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Event (per WelcomeBC)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Jan 31, 2025</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2025 allocation confirmed at 4,000, a 50% reduction from 2024</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Oct 2, 2025</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,254 additional nominations for 2025</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Dec 15, 2025</td><td class="px-4 py-3">960 more; final 2025 allocation 6,214</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Dec 18, 2025</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2026 allocation set at 5,254; BC had requested 9,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Aug 18, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,000 additional; 2026 allocation 6,254, to be used by December 31, 2026</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-amber-50"><td class="px-4 py-3">Nov 11, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No 2027 allocation announced yet</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        By our arithmetic, BC's current 6,254 is about 6.8% of the 2026 national PNP target of 91,500. <strong>Illustration only, not a forecast:</strong> applying the same share to the new 2027 target of 95,000 gives roughly 6,500. The one-time measure adds a second variable, because IRCC says provinces will nominate in-Canada workers under expanded PNP allocations, but it has not said how many spaces each province gets. In practice, BC's opening allocation has been well below its final allocation in both recent years. Allocations are negotiated, and BC has said it "continues to advocate" for more. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-2027-outlook-predictions" class="text-blue-600 underline">BC PNP 2027 outlook</a> looks at what different allocation outcomes would mean for draws.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The Express Entry piece</h2>

      <p>
        The Levels Plan sets <em>how many</em> people come through Express Entry. The categories decide <em>who</em> gets invited. IRCC ran a separate consultation from August 4 to September 1, 2026 on 2027 categories. It asked about expanding efforts to attract highly skilled workers, <em>narrowing</em> the number or eligibility of labour-shortage categories, and keeping the Francophone focus. Separately, IRCC's Express Entry scoring overhaul took effect on November 3, 2026: the maximum CRS is now 1,270, a new labour market integration factor is worth up to 150 points, and a qualifying job offer no longer needs an LMIA for Federal Skilled Worker and Federal Skilled Trades candidates invited on or after that date. Rounds were paused while IRCC recalculates scores, and no 2027 category list had been announced as of November 11, 2026. With Federal High Skilled cut to 100,000, provincial nomination becomes relatively more important. For BC PNP candidates using the <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-express-entry-bc-eebc-guide-2026" class="text-blue-600 underline">Express Entry BC option</a>, the provincial nomination still matters most. Read our <a href="/articles/express-entry-2027-category-changes" class="text-blue-600 underline">Express Entry 2027 guide</a> for the category details.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The three scenarios we outlined before release (analysis)</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Scenario</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">What it would look like</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">What it would mean for BC PNP candidates</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Status quo</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2027 notional targets confirmed; small tweaks to TR numbers</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Allocation likely similar in scale to 2026; Care/Build/Innovate draws continue at a similar pace</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Tighter</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Lower TR arrivals and/or a lower economic or PNP line to hit the 5% goal</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Fewer nominations and higher cutoffs; work-permit timing becomes urgent</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Transition-heavy</td><td class="px-4 py-3">PR total held, but more space reserved for people already in Canada</td><td class="px-4 py-3">In-Canada workers with B.C. job offers benefit; candidates abroad face more competition</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Before the release we said the public record supported the first and third scenarios more than the second. The published plan is closest to a mix of those two: the 2027 totals match the previous plan, the PNP line is slightly higher, and the one-time 2027 measure reserves space for people already in Canada. It did not follow the "tighter" path. What it means for BC in practice depends on the 2027 allocation, which has not been announced.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What to do now</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Do not wait for BC's 2027 allocation to register.</strong> BC PNP registration is free and your place in the pool depends on your score, not the federal plan. Check your score with our <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP calculator</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Map your status expiry dates.</strong> If your work permit ends in 2027, plan around the slow start to BC PNP draws seen in both 2025 and 2026, and watch for IRCC and BC details on the one-time measure for workers already in Canada.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep language tests valid.</strong> BC PNP results are valid for two years and must be valid at registration to earn points.</li>
        <li><strong>Budget for fees.</strong> The BC PNP application fee is $1,750. See our <a href="/articles/canada-pr-application-fees-2027" class="text-blue-600 underline">PR fees 2027 guide</a> for federal costs.</li>
        <li><strong>Have a second route.</strong> If your occupation is not a BC priority, compare other provinces in our <a href="/articles/best-provinces-pnp-2027" class="text-blue-600 underline">best provinces for PNP 2027</a> guide.</li>
      </ul>

